[{:db/id 17592186083325 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-979b0f60-49c9-5b48-86f8-6e501f45d940"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-979b0f60-49c9-5b48-86f8-6e501f45d940" :question/id #uuid "979b0f60-49c9-5b48-86f8-6e501f45d940" :question/math-academy-id "q-95151" :question/problem "The average duration (in minutes) of the videos produced by a popular content creator is $35\\,\\text{min}.$ Also, $5%$ of their videos have a duration of less than $20$ minutes. Assuming that the duration of the videos is normally distributed, find the standard deviation of the duration times, rounded to the near minute.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the duration of a video. Then we have $X∼N(35,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 20)=0.05.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 20) & =0.05 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{20-35}{\\sigma }) & =0.05 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{15}{\\sigma }) & =0.05 \\\\ -\\frac{15}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.05).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.05)=-1.6449.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{15}{\\sigma } & =-1.6449 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.1097 \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈9\\,\\text{minutes}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0b712d39-ff45-54f8-b106-03be0d0cda60"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0b712d39-ff45-54f8-b106-03be0d0cda60" :answer-field/id #uuid "0b712d39-ff45-54f8-b106-03be0d0cda60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-01aac47c-bc41-58aa-a4b6-55ca3bbd11b0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-58d37435-1075-579f-a1f9-ce0f3925f1fb" "answer-f1fee109-4588-538f-b649-9c3fd53fe5ef" "answer-4e4b0043-0c74-53a6-a993-6328a52b0af3" "answer-2ef719c3-66a7-50b4-a2f7-e921efeb034a" "answer-01aac47c-bc41-58aa-a4b6-55ca3bbd11b0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58d37435-1075-579f-a1f9-ce0f3925f1fb" :answer/id #uuid "58d37435-1075-579f-a1f9-ce0f3925f1fb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$11$ minutes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1fee109-4588-538f-b649-9c3fd53fe5ef" :answer/id #uuid "f1fee109-4588-538f-b649-9c3fd53fe5ef" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$12$ minutes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e4b0043-0c74-53a6-a993-6328a52b0af3" :answer/id #uuid "4e4b0043-0c74-53a6-a993-6328a52b0af3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$14$ minutes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ef719c3-66a7-50b4-a2f7-e921efeb034a" :answer/id #uuid "2ef719c3-66a7-50b4-a2f7-e921efeb034a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$8$ minutes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01aac47c-bc41-58aa-a4b6-55ca3bbd11b0" :answer/id #uuid "01aac47c-bc41-58aa-a4b6-55ca3bbd11b0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$9$ minutes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083326 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-384a66a5-a5c9-58f8-aa5d-723b50b59cc7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-384a66a5-a5c9-58f8-aa5d-723b50b59cc7" :question/id #uuid "384a66a5-a5c9-58f8-aa5d-723b50b59cc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-325130" :question/problem "The fasting glucose level of a clinic’s patients is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu \\,\\text{mg/dL}$ and a standard deviation of $15\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$ Additionally, $60%$ of the patients have a glucose level between $0.92\\mu \\,\\text{mg/dL}$ and $1.08\\mu \\,\\text{mg/dL}.$ Find the percentage of patients with a fasting glucose lower than $130\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$ **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0262\\,$ | $\\,0.0256\\,$ | $\\,0.0250\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0329\\,$ | $\\,0.0322\\,$ | $\\,0.0314\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0409\\,$ | $\\,0.0401\\,$ | $\\,0.0392\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the patients have a fasting glucose lower than $130\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the fasting glucose level of a randomly selected patient. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{15}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<130).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.92\\mu <X<1.08\\mu )=0.60.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.92\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.30 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.5+0.30 \\\\ P(X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.80 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.08\\mu }{15}) & =0.80 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.08\\mu }{15}) & =0.80 \\\\ \\frac{0.08\\mu }{15} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.80).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.80)=0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.08\\mu }{15} & =0.8416 \\\\ 0.08\\mu  & =12.624 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{12.624}{0.08} \\\\  & ≈157.80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<130)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<130) & =P(Z<\\frac{130-157.80}{15}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.85) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.85) \\\\  & =0.0322 \\\\  & ≈3.22%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $3.22%$ of the patients have a fasting glucose lower than $130\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3ee986dc-58c5-5a6b-9a45-c8daf6a2b863"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3ee986dc-58c5-5a6b-9a45-c8daf6a2b863" :answer-field/id #uuid "3ee986dc-58c5-5a6b-9a45-c8daf6a2b863" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-83221c93-603d-5671-86b0-b2c0e8b223f2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-83221c93-603d-5671-86b0-b2c0e8b223f2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83221c93-603d-5671-86b0-b2c0e8b223f2" :answer/id #uuid "83221c93-603d-5671-86b0-b2c0e8b223f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.22" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-2c1f6cc5-5968-538f-9311-8522a9dd360b" :activity/id #uuid "2c1f6cc5-5968-538f-9311-8522a9dd360b" :activity/title "Modeling With the Normal Distribution" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050198 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-08ba30f8-c04b-524e-80f7-0ec881803edf" "step-cd1c27a9-4fc5-505a-a0b1-c103e40a7537" "step-13582afe-72fb-5625-8464-5f459b09da11" "step-b1290537-191a-5941-898f-4165f87ba9c8" "step-4bf19974-65f5-541f-96e4-687af8d2f431"] :activity/first-step "step-08ba30f8-c04b-524e-80f7-0ec881803edf" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-08ba30f8-c04b-524e-80f7-0ec881803edf" :step/id #uuid "08ba30f8-c04b-524e-80f7-0ec881803edf" :step/content "ques